## 5. Measuring the Risk

### 5.1 What I measured

- **Volatility.** The annual standard deviation of returns for the whole portfolio, calculated from the weights, volatilities and correlations.
- **Beta.** How much the portfolio moves for a 1% move in the S&P 500.
- **Value at Risk (VaR), 95%, one year.** The loss that should only be exceeded in about 1 year out of 20. I used the normal-distribution formula: VaR = -(expected return - 1.645 x volatility).
- **Conditional VaR (CVaR).** The average loss in the worst 5% of years. It tells you how bad the bad years are, not just where they start.
- **Sharpe ratio.** Return above cash divided by volatility, using 4% as the cash return.
- **Risk contribution.** Each asset's share of the portfolio's total variance. It shows where the risk really comes from, which can differ a lot from where the money is.

### 5.2 Results

| Measure | Portfolio A (concentrated) | Portfolio B (diversified) |
| --- | ---: | ---: |
| Expected annual return | 7.4% | 6.1% |
| Volatility | 18.3% | 10.8% |
| Beta against the S&P 500 | 1.07 | 0.65 |
| VaR, 95%, one year | 22.8%  ($22,758) | 11.8%  ($11,771) |
| CVaR (average loss in worst 5% of years) | 30.4%  ($30,407) | 16.3%  ($16,298) |
| Sharpe ratio | 0.19 | 0.19 |
| Diversification ratio | 1.02 | 1.26 |
| Share of risk from US technology | 83.8% | 27.8% |

*Table 6. Risk metrics. The diversification ratio is the weighted average of individual volatilities divided by the portfolio volatility; a figure near 1 means almost no diversification benefit.*

The headline is how much the risk fell and how little the expected return did. Moving from A to B cut volatility by 41% (from 18.3% to 10.8%), cut the one-year VaR by 48% and cut the average loss in the worst years by 46%. The price was 1.3 percentage points of expected return. Beta fell from 1.07 to 0.65.

The Sharpe ratio barely moved (0.19 against 0.19). That means diversification did not make the portfolio worse per unit of risk. It simply let it take far less risk. Under my assumptions, technology compensates you only slightly better than the broad market for its extra volatility, so concentrating in it does not pay for itself.

Portfolio A is the clearest example of why risk contribution matters. Technology is 70% of the money but about 84% of the risk. The diversification ratio of 1.02 is close to 1, which tells me the portfolio is almost a single bet. In Portfolio B no sleeve contributes more than about 42% of the risk, and bonds and gold, which are 35% of the money, add only about 9% of the risk, which is exactly their job.

