### 5.3 Worked examples

To show where the numbers in Table 6 come from, here are the calculations for Portfolio A and Portfolio B written out.

| Calculation | Working | Result |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Expected return, Portfolio A | 70% x 8.0% + 20% x 7.0% + 10% x 4.0% | 5.60 + 1.40 + 0.40 = 7.40% |
| Expected return, Portfolio B | 15% x 8.0% + 30% x 7.0% + 15% x 6.5% + 25% x 5.0% + 10% x 3.5% + 5% x 4.0% | 1.20 + 2.10 + 0.98 + 1.25 + 0.35 + 0.20 = 6.08% |
| Variance of A (technology and broad equity; cash adds almost nothing) | 0.70^2 x 0.22^2 + 0.20^2 x 0.16^2 + 2 x 0.70 x 0.20 x 0.90 x 0.22 x 0.16 | 0.023716 + 0.001024 + 0.008870 = 0.033610 |
| Volatility of A | Square root of 0.033610 | 18.33% |
| One-year VaR at 95%, A | -(7.40% - 1.645 x 18.33%) | 22.75%, or $22,750 on $100,000 |
| Loss in the 2008 scenario, A | 70% x (-42%) + 20% x (-37%) + 10% x (+2%) | -29.40 - 7.40 + 0.20 = -36.6% |
| Loss in the 2008 scenario, B | 15% x (-42) + 30% x (-37) + 15% x (-43) + 25% x 5 + 10% x 5 + 5% x 2 | -6.30 - 11.10 - 6.45 + 1.25 + 0.50 + 0.10 = -22.0% |

*Table 6a. Risk calculations written out.*

The correlation of 0.90 between technology and broad equity is the reason the cross term (0.008870) is so large. If the two were uncorrelated, the cross term would be zero and the volatility of A would fall to about 15.7%. Because they are highly correlated, mixing them barely reduces risk, which is the central point of the week.

