### 6.2 Monte Carlo simulation

The stress tests show only four paths. To see a wider range I simulated 200,000 possible one-year outcomes for each portfolio. The simulation draws returns from the assumptions in Tables 4 and 5, but it uses a Student-t distribution with 5 degrees of freedom instead of a normal curve, so that extreme years happen more often than a bell curve allows. I set the random seed to 2026 so that the numbers can be reproduced.

| One-year result | Portfolio A | Portfolio B |
| --- | ---: | ---: |
| Average return | 7.4% | 6.1% |
| Return in the 5th percentile (1 year in 20 is worse) | -21.2% | -10.8% |
| Return in the 1st percentile (1 year in 100 is worse) | -40.0% | -22.1% |
| Average of the worst 5% of years | -33.4% | -18.1% |
| Probability of any loss | 31.1% | 25.0% |
| Probability of losing more than 10% | 13.7% | 5.7% |
| Probability of losing more than 20% | 5.6% | 1.3% |

*Table 8. Monte Carlo results, 200,000 simulated years each.*

In the simulation, Portfolio A loses more than 10% in about 14 years out of 100, and more than 20% in about 6. Portfolio B loses more than 10% in about 6 years out of 100 and more than 20% in about 1. The probability of simply losing money in a year only falls from about 31% to 25%, which is a useful reminder that diversification does not remove losing years. It shrinks the size of the losses. The one-year VaR from the simulation (21.2% for A and 10.8% for B) is close to the formula-based numbers in Table 6, which gives me some confidence that the two methods are consistent.

