| US technology | 70% | 15% | -55 pts | -$55,000 | Keeps exposure to the AI and software themes I researched in Weeks 1 to 3 but stops it dominating. Note that the S&P 500 holds a large technology weight too, so the real technology exposure in B is higher than 15%. |
| US broad equity | 20% | 30% | +10 pts | +$10,000 | The core equity holding. Wide spread across companies and sectors. |
| International developed equity | 0% | 15% | +15 pts | +$15,000 | Adds exposure to other economies, currencies and sector mixes. It is still equity risk, so it helps less in a global sell-off than other assets do. |
| US investment-grade bonds | 0% | 25% | +25 pts | +$25,000 | Gives income at current yields and a cushion in growth-driven sell-offs (it worked in 2000 to 2002 and 2008). It did not help in 2022. |
| Gold | 0% | 10% | +10 pts | +$10,000 | Held for crisis and inflation protection. It rose or held up in all but one scenario here, and had little connection to technology in my assumptions. It pays no income. |
| Cash | 10% | 5% | -5 pts | -$5,000 | Provides stability, liquidity for rebalancing, and a return of about 4% at current policy rates. |

