### 7.2 How I would get there

1. **Move in stages.** Selling $55,000 of technology in a single day is a timing decision in itself. I would shift in three to six tranches so that no single day decides the result. If selling triggers tax, that cost should be weighed first.
2. **Use index funds for the technology sleeve.** This keeps single-company risk, the Microsoft-type drawdown, out of the portfolio.
3. **Cap individual stocks.** No single stock above about 5% of the portfolio.
4. **Split the bond sleeve.** Hold a mix of short-to-intermediate Treasuries and inflation-linked bonds, so that a repeat of 2022 hurts less. Avoid very long maturities while yields are still rising.
5. **Rebalance by rule.** Check at least once a year, and also whenever any asset class drifts more than 5 percentage points from its target. This forces selling what has run up and buying what has lagged, without relying on opinion.
6. **Keep an emergency fund outside the portfolio.** Three to six months of expenses, so that a bad market never forces selling.
7. **Avoid leverage.** Borrowing to invest turns a bad year into a forced sale.
8. **Optional hedging.** For anyone who must hold a large single stock, a protective put or a collar can limit losses, at a cost. I would treat it as an exception, not the standard.

